Z||000000||fyerstarter||05-13-2001||10:44 PM||||I've been having a high idle problem for some time now. So I decided to block of the EGR and see if it helped so I did that (at the manifold and the exhaust with litte tin plates) and started it up only to find that it was idling higher! around 1700. SO I figured I should undo my changes and found that I had accidentally unplugged the vacuum line going to the throttle body which was prob the cause. Anyway to make a long story short I plugged that back in, decided just to go back to the way it was for now and started it up. NOW to my surprise the check engine light is permanantly on and the idle is totally ****ed. It will erratically jump up to 2000 and and then back to 1000 and its all over the place. Checked the codes and I got a 34,35, and 42 (Ignition Timing) so I figure I must have ****ed up something when I took off the dist cap to mess with the EGR tube. But Im not sure what I could have done. I checked everything and it all looks good, I never even removed the wires from the cap so I know they are in the right place, all the wires going to the module, coil, etc. look good. <P>Does anyone have any suggestions what to look for?<P>Im getting a code 34 (MAP sensor), 35 (Idle) and 42 (timing). Before I was getting a 32 (EGR) and 35. <P>I just replaced the MAP sensor two weeks ago and I dont think the old one was bad. So maybe this new one if toast so Ill prob put in the old one to see. But what about the timing thing?<P>A few things I noticed. <P>The one of the little wires going to the module inside the cap is wearing a little thin. Could that cause it?<P>The car still starts and runs, just crappily. <P>Ahhh my head hurts. Does anyone have any hints on where to start troubleshooting this mess? I promise I'll mail you a beer next day air to whoever help me figure out the problem  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Thanks a million guys.||||reg||8
Z||000001||87GTZ34||05-14-2001||01:07 AM||||Well the 1st thing I'd do after the vacuum exercise is to make sure everything is back where you started then disconnect the battery and go back to square 1.  The idle and map codes may have been caused by the vacuum conditions.<P>GL||||reg||1
Z||000002||btoth||05-14-2001||12:52 PM||||Make sure that you didn't forget to connect the vacuum line to the EGR valve if you were messing with it.  Or didn't break the vacuum line going to it.  I forgot to connect mine once and my car idled at over 2000RPM.  (Of course, I could hear the hissing vacuum noise comming from it.)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||05-14-2001||12:54 PM||||Also check the vacuum lines.  The ones on my car are getting so brittle they break VERY easily.  Gonna be time for Rodney's metal replacements pretty soon.||||reg||6
